Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 23:03:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 7/8] WP-0.7, enforcement (cld-advisor's series 4, patch 7;
 built on 323f92327 with series 2, series 3 and the first six patches of
 series 4 applied): A LOCK DOES NOT COMPARE, AS TEXT, A FLOAT THAT A PROCESS
 PRINTED (the seat's planted case PV26). The last digits a process prints for
 a float depend on the machine and its libraries. A lock that compares them as
 text passes where it was written and can fail on a clean checkout elsewhere,
 for no fault of the result; four locks on main do so on the machine that
 found the class. No gate read the form. THE MEND: the checker of the vacuity
 gate reads it from the lock's syntax tree. The form is a test that starts a
 process, itself or through a function or a fixture of its file or through a
 name its file binds to what a process gave, and asserts on text that holds a
 float with six or more digits after the point, or with an exponent, where
 that part of the assert is about what the process gave. The text is a
 literal, a text constant of the module, or a committed file that the assert
 compares for equality with what the process gave. In such a file a zero
 printed with a sign is read as a float too: it keeps the sign of what it was
 before rounding, and one lock on main, written to hold integers and eighth
 roots only, fails here by three such signs. A part of an assert that says
 something is not there pins nothing; a process whose command is git is not
 counted. A test of the form that is not named fails the gate, with the mend
 in its sentence: read the number out and compare it as a number. Fifteen
 tests stood on the day of the rule, six that compare a whole transcript and
 nine that hold a literal; they are held by name as found, and are main's to
 mend. The gate's sentence counts them, counts the whole transcripts it does
 not refuse because no such number stands in them (seven on this tree), and
 counts an assert that compares what a process gave with a file the gate could
 not find. What it does not reach: whether a printed float is stable is not in
 the commit, so the gate reads a form and not a fault; a float printed inside
 the test's own process and captured; a process started in another module; a
 float of fewer digits; text compared through a helper. The lock: six tests
 added to tests/test_a_gate_reads_all_it_is_about.py, ten ways of pinning and
 eight that are not the form. Tested against planted errors, 10 of 10 ways;
 computed once. 0 of 19.

